# Cloudflare generates the deploy-time secret prompts from this file, so only
# variables the *deployed Worker* actually reads belong here as active keys.
# Anything commented out is documentation, not a prompt.

# ── Required at runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 32+ character random string. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Without this, Better Auth refuses to start.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""

# Deployed origin, e.g. https://go.example.com. Safe to leave blank at deploy
# time — /setup records the real origin, and the request origin is used until
# then.
ORIGIN=""

# ── Optional at runtime — add later under Settings > Variables & Secrets ──────
#
# Analytics dashboard. Both are required together; without them the analytics
# page renders a "not configured" notice and scans are still recorded.
#   CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN=""      # API token with Account Analytics read
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=""   # only used to query Analytics Engine
#
# Email. Defaults to Cloudflare Email Sending, which needs Workers Paid.
#   EMAIL_PROVIDER=""          # cloudflare (default) | resend | none
#   EMAIL_FROM=""              # sender address for magic links + invites
#   RESEND_API_KEY=""          # only when EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend

# ── Local tooling only — NOT read by the deployed Worker ──────────────────────
#
# drizzle-kit talks to D1 over the HTTP API, so it needs credentials that the
# Worker never does: at runtime D1 is reached through the `DB` binding in
# wrangler.jsonc. Put these in .env (gitignored) for local migration commands.
# They are commented out so they do not appear as deploy prompts.
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=""
#   CLOUDFLARE_DATABASE_ID=""
#   CLOUDFLARE_D1_TOKEN=""
